\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Our approach to AI\
There's a very clear decision for marketers to make that will shape the future of their careers. There are those who are choosing not to challenge themselves, choosing not to throw themselves into an AI self-development journey, and they are going to get increasingly left behind. There are others who are being proactive and really leaning into that self-development.\
This feels like a lot. I get it. But what I want you to do is not feel bitten by that feeling, but instead choose to embrace it \'97 because if we let the overwhelm put us off, we're just falling into that camp of getting left behind.\
Mindset is the key differentiator. The tech is going to catch up to make the UX increasingly accessible for every individual. The difference isn't about whether you're technically advanced, but whether you are enthusiastic and open-minded.\
We're going to be increasingly forced to think in a systematic way, so there is a new skill for the blue-sky, big-picture brains among us: turning our approach into something methodical. We're going to become prompt officers, managers of tokens and computing bandwidth. That is a realistic representation of the future requirements on our role. The curve is getting ever steeper, and the difference between those who are already being proactive and those who are fighting the rising tides is going to get more and more significant. I am now surprised to see job ads that don't specifically call out either proven AI expertise or, at the very least, evidence of real proactivity and a hunger to learn.\
There are countless ways in which I am working leagues faster than I did six months ago, and the impact of that on my productivity and output is evidence to me of just how big this gap is going to get between those who are not embracing this at all. And I'm so painfully aware of all of the time I'm still wasting by not being truly best in class at this stuff.\
What leaning in actually looks like is finding the time and the energy to play. In a time of economic uncertainty and a really pressurised job market, this does have to happen outside the 9-5. That is an expectation on a marketer who wants to future-proof their own development.\
How you do this can be very individual. Whether that's joining community events to be part of the conversation, finding AI study groups to dedicate time to play, or taking on personal automation projects and challenges to build up your confidence \'97 there is a different solution for whatever your learning style is.\
On the question of authenticity: to hold the view that AI is going to make work feel less like yours is to be very reductive in how it can be used. There is a very valid conversation to be had about only using AI to turn you into a superhuman \'97 to do all of the admin and repeatable tasks and heavy lifting so that your magic brain can do the truly magical stuff. But I'm also really experimenting with the possibility that AI can help us do stuff better than we currently can. Yes, that's terrifying. But to be held back by that terror and not explore that idea in full is going to be hugely limiting.\
Up World is pedalling fast to build out a programme of talks and content that supports the AI learning and development journey for marketers with all of these different learning styles. There are already Huddle Ups happening every week with AI at the heart of the conversation. There is an AI Wins and Fails challenge where we are openly navigating this together. There is lots of great stuff coming, including an AI course.\
